{"text": "Category R is Behaviour Support or Mental Illness. The educational description is: A student with persistent moderate behaviour-support needs or a diagnosed mental illness whose frequency or severity significantly disrupts learning, relationships, or personal adjustment across settings despite usual classroom supports. This description is about educational impact and the support required for meaningful participation. It should not be used as a prediction of intelligence, personality, or future potential.\n\nOur fictional student is Lucas Tremblay, who is in Grade 5 and uses he/him pronouns. Lucas has generalized anxiety disorder and persistent school avoidance with emotional escalation across home and school. He has not responded to ordinary classroom management alone and receives a coordinated moderate support plan. This background information matters because it connects the designation to observable access needs at school.\n\nWhen we plan for Lucas, we ask three practical questions: What can the student already do? What barriers are interfering with participation or learning? And what support will remove those barriers while building independence? Those questions keep the profile individualized and useful. Planning should include the student, family, teacher, case manager, and relevant specialists.\n\nI will now hand the presentation to Presenter 2, who will begin with Lucas’s strengths and communication.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-R-02.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Category", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.725, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "R", "start": 0.7375, "end": 1.0875, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "is", "start": 1.1, "end": 1.3, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "Behaviour", "start": 1.3125, "end": 1.8125, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 23}, {"text": "Support", "start": 1.825, "end": 2.4375, "start_char": 24, "end_char": 31}, {"text": "or", "start": 2.45, "end": 2.5625, "start_char": 32, "e